## Chapter 14: Allocators: The Core Abstraction

If you’ve spent time in C, you know `malloc` and `free` as global, implicit functions that grab memory from the heap. If you come from Rust, you’re used to the global allocator being hidden behind `Box` and `Vec`, with custom allocators still in the experimental `allocator_api` wilderness. In Zig, the allocator is not a global or a language runtime feature—it is an explicit, ordinary value that you pass around like any other dependency.

This chapter dissects `std.mem.Allocator`, the interface that powers every allocation in the standard library and in well‑structured Zig code. You’ll learn how to use the built‑in allocators, what happens inside the vtable, why Zig forces you to spell out every allocation, and how to write your own allocators that obey the contract.

---

### 14.1 Basic Usage

The `std.mem.Allocator` interface is defined by a small set of methods:
- `alloc` – allocate memory for `n` items of type `T`
- `create` – allocate and initialize a single value
- `free` – release memory back to the allocator
- `destroy` – free a single value created with `create`
- `resize` – attempt to change the size of an existing allocation (optional)
- `realloc` – reallocate, falling back to alloc/copy/free if resize fails

Every function that needs to allocate memory in idiomatic Zig accepts an `Allocator` parameter. The standard library provides several ready‑to‑use allocators.

```zig
const std = @import("std");

pub fn main() !void {
    // 1. The page allocator – asks the OS for whole pages. Slow, but trivial.
    const page_allocator = std.heap.page_allocator;
    const slice = try page_allocator.alloc(u8, 4096);
    defer page_allocator.free(slice);

    // 2. GeneralPurposeAllocator – production ready, safety, leak detection.
    var gpa = std.heap.GeneralPurposeAllocator(.{}){};
    defer _ = gpa.deinit();  // reports leaks
    const gpa_allocator = gpa.allocator();

    const int_ptr = try gpa_allocator.create(i32);
    defer gpa_allocator.destroy(int_ptr);
    int_ptr.* = 42;

    // 3. Arena allocator – allocates many objects, frees all at once.
    var arena = std.heap.ArenaAllocator.init(gpa_allocator);
    defer arena.deinit();
    const arena_allocator = arena.allocator();

    const many_bytes = try arena_allocator.alloc(u8, 1024);
    // no need to free each allocation – arena.deinit() frees everything.

    // 4. FixedBufferAllocator – allocate from a static buffer (stack or global).
    var buffer: [2000]u8 = undefined;
    var fba = std.heap.FixedBufferAllocator.init(&buffer);
    const fba_allocator = fba.allocator();

    const fixed_slice = try fba_allocator.alloc(u8, 500);
    // fba_allocator.free(fixed_slice) is optional – you may reset the whole buffer.
}
```

All allocation functions return error unions (`![]T` or `!*T`) because allocation can fail. Every successful `alloc` or `create` must be paired with a `free` or `destroy`, or you leak memory. The `defer` statement is the conventional tool for pairing these operations.

